<section class="concept-card">
    <h2>Topic</h2>
    <p><strong>G.A.B.E.</strong> — A structured academic framework for capturing, organizing, and reinforcing knowledge by treating life's complexities like a polynomial system.</p>
</section>

<section class="concept-card">
    <h2>Definition</h2>
    <p>
        Gabe’s Note-Taking Framework is a method for capturing the <strong>multiple variables</strong> of any subject and organizing them so their <strong>intersection</strong> becomes clear. 
        Just as a polynomial $y = a_nx^n + ... + a_1x + a_0$ has many terms that combine to create a single outcome ($y$), G.A.B.E. helps you identify each "term" (variable) in a lecture or text, and map how they intersect to define your final understanding.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="concept-card">
    <h2>Rule: The G.A.B.E. Acronym</h2>
    <p>Each letter represents a step to isolate and combine the "terms" of your learning.</p>
    <ul class="acronym-list">
        <li><span class="acronym-letter">G</span><span class="acronym-def"><strong>Get</strong> the Leading Term — Identify the core concept or main variable ($x$) that drives the equation. What is the primary outcome we are solving for?</span></li>
        <li><span class="acronym-letter">A</span><span class="acronym-def"><strong>Annotate</strong> the Coefficients — Write down the details, definitions, and formulas ($a_n, a_{n-1}...$) that modify the main idea. These are the constants that change the shape of the curve.</span></li>
        <li><span class="acronym-letter">B</span><span class="acronym-def"><strong>Build</strong> the Intersections — Connect the new terms to prior knowledge. Draw arrows between variables to show how they intersect, multiply, or cancel each other out.</span></li>
        <li><span class="acronym-letter">E</span><span class="acronym-def"><strong>Echo</strong> the Result — Summarize or teach the final outcome ($y$). If you can't solve for $y$ (the main point) using your notes, the polynomial is incomplete.</span></li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="concept-card">
    <h2>Example: Applying G.A.B.E. to Logarithms</h2>
    <p>
        Imagine a lecture on <strong>Exponential Growth vs. Logarithmic Decay</strong>.
    </p>
    <p><strong>G (Get):</strong> The main variable is <em>time ($t$)</em>. The outcome ($y$) is the amount of substance remaining.</p>
    <p><strong>A (Annotate):</strong> 
        <br>Term 1: $A_0$ (Initial amount)
        <br>Term 2: $k$ (Decay constant)
        <br>Formula: $A(t) = A_0 e^{-kt}$
    </p>
    <p><strong>B (Build):</strong> Connect this to <em>Half-Life</em>. Draw an arrow from $k$ to $t_{1/2} = \ln(2)/k$. Notice how the log function <em>inverts</em> the exponential "term" to isolate $t$.
    </p>
    <p><strong>E (Echo):</strong> "The final outcome ($y$) depends on how the initial amount and decay rate intersect over time. The logarithm is just the tool we use to solve for the time variable when we know the outcome."</p>
</section>

